WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.270
Film Courage: In the beginning of your filmmaking
career, how fearful or brave were you to ask

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:07.170
for more?

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:12.670
Rachel Grady: More from our subjects, more
money?

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:13.830
Film Courage: Everything, everything.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:19.430
I know you had talked about an interview on
confidence versus being afraid to ask for

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:20.430
more.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:25.302
And that really, a light bulb went off, not
even that it was a gender specific idea [“women

00:00:25.302 --> 00:00:28.380
should ask for more”] but just the fear
of appearing too…

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.320
Rachel Grady: …Pushy?

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.820
It is gender-specific.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:34.820
Film Courage: It is?

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.540
Okay…But I think you mentioned how you didn’t
realize that was a thing [stereotype] and

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:40.470
that you both were okay asking for more.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:49.870
Rachel Grady: I think a lot of women (professional
women) have that kind of trajectory which

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:57.880
is (you know) as you have more success and
you have more personal confidence, you will

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.130
ask for more.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:04.629
And we’re two women, we own our own company,
we have staff, and we’ve noticed over the

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:11.770
years that the boy employees will always ask
for more, even if they don’t deserve it.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:18.939
And the women won’t, so I think part of
it is just that we matured and we got more

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:22.619
confident but also we are business owners
and I think you see the world differently

00:01:22.619 --> 00:01:27.170
when you are asking for money, when you are
taking care of money, when you have staff

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:30.939
working for you, when you’re a director,
when you’re telling people what your vision

00:01:30.939 --> 00:01:34.720
is and expecting them…they work for you.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:41.060
So all of the things that help people get
more confident and ask for more, we were put

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:46.179
in a position that we were able to grow in
it.

00:01:46.179 --> 00:01:52.759
So now that we’re almost 30, we’re insanely
confident.

00:01:52.759 --> 00:02:03.069
Heidi Ewing: It is a privilege to make documentary
films, it is a privilege to be in this business

00:02:03.069 --> 00:02:07.490
and direct, but there’s also a marketplace
for the stories we have to tell, there is

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.869
more of a marketplace than there has ever
been.

00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:14.500
And I think a lot of filmmakers, especially
earlier in their career just feel so lucky

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.900
that anyone is interested in financing their
work or supporting their work and I totally

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:19.939
understand that.

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:25.950
But I think it’s really important to graduate
into a place where you realize that everyone

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.370
is moved by important stories well told.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.670
And it’s hard to tell a story (an important
story) well.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:36.209
And so there’s a premium for those people
who can do it.

00:02:36.209 --> 00:02:42.920
And it’s important to realize that especially
as a woman director, because if you don’t

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.530
ask, it will not be offered to you.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:51.750
So you have to know that and sort of make
demands that are reasonable, that are fair,

00:02:51.750 --> 00:03:01.290
so you don’t just end up working for less
or feeling less than.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.580
So that happened pretty early on in our career.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:09.030
I mean we put our heads down and we work and
we make a movie, that’s what we do.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:15.440
But we also know that this is important work
that people want to see and will pay for and

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:16.440
that sort of thing.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.930
So it’s really finding that balance between
doing subjects that really interest us and

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:23.370
also realizing that it’s a business as well.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:28.489
Film Courage: So then asking for more, being
given somewhat access to FootSteps [the clinic

00:03:28.489 --> 00:03:31.239
that was in their documentary ONE OF US],
I think you said that they let you sort of

00:03:31.239 --> 00:03:34.040
be in their lobby for a little bit, it was
like baby steps with them?

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.359
Rachel Grady: No, that was the access that
they gave us.

00:03:36.359 --> 00:03:37.359
Heidi Ewing: That was it.

00:03:37.359 --> 00:03:41.750
Rachel Grady: They gave us the ability to
meet their clients.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:43.599
That’s what we were asking them for.

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:47.310
Heidi Ewing: We did not film inside Footsteps
hardly ever, except a couple of one-on-one

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.750
sessions which you see in the movie.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:49.750
Film Courage: Right.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:50.750
And they [those scenes in ONE OF US movie}
are excellent, by the way.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:51.750
Rachel Grady: Thank you.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:52.750
Heidi Ewing: Those were hard-fought scenes.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:53.750
Thank you!

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:55.021
Those were very, very hard to get.

00:03:55.021 --> 00:03:56.319
Film Courage: I can see why.

00:03:56.319 --> 00:04:01.799
Rachel Grady: So we weren’t asking them
for more, that’s all we needed from them.

00:04:01.799 --> 00:04:10.519
We were very persistent and patient, and gave
them a good pitch of why we were the right

00:04:10.519 --> 00:04:17.090
people for them to trust and they did it and
then we met their members and we kept our

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:23.961
end of the bargain and we made a movie that
they like and they can use in any way that

00:04:23.961 --> 00:04:26.780
they want.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:32.370
We all got something out of it, which is another
thing that you understand as you get more

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:35.910
seasoned that everyone is doing something
for their own incentive.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:38.920
And you have to figure out what that incentive
is.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:44.000
Heidi Ewing: And also in terms of asking for
more, I mean they had been contacted by multiple

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.360
filmmakers over the year and they had said
“no.”

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:51.160
And so we said, but you haven’t been contacted
by us and we have a body of work to show.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.389
So it’s not abstract so much.

00:04:53.389 --> 00:04:57.720
They could see all of our past films, our
five plus feature-length documentaries plus

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.919
plus a lot of other work and they can judge
for themselves how big of a risk is this.

00:05:02.919 --> 00:05:03.919
Footsteps took a big risk.

00:05:03.919 --> 00:05:05.760
They had no editorial control over the film.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:11.090
They were not invited for any editorial feedback
and so they had to trust that at least these

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:15.060
filmmakers didn’t have a specific agenda
and would tell it like it is, which is all

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:16.060
we can guarantee.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:20.570
And so they’ve kind of taken leaps as well.

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:22.889
And that’s what it takes.

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:26.940
It takes really, really brave individuals
that will trust you and organizations in order

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:29.250
for this documentary thing to even happen.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:36.190
And we rely on people to trust us and we rely
on goodwill and we take it as a big responsibility

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:37.729
and big gift.

00:05:37.729 --> 00:05:42.000
And it can be a heavy burden because people
have trusted you and entrusted you with their

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.720
secrets and their stories and so that has
not gotten easier.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:50.210
That is just something that we grapple with
every time.

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:53.900
Are we doing right by this person?

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.889
Did this person mean to say that?

00:05:55.889 --> 00:05:59.591
Or is this the one day that they would say
such a crazy thing that would be great in

00:05:59.591 --> 00:06:01.370
the movie but they actually really don’t
mean it.

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:07.160
So you have to sort of also suss out (you
know) what is representative of a person or

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:08.160
an organization.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:15.160
So we had to grapple with a lot of those sort
of rocky moments on this particular movie.

